\begin{lemma}\label{exterior}
If a graph $H$ of girth at least $5$ has $t$ vertices and degree sequence
$d_1,\ldots,d_t$, then
\[
 t(t-1)\ge\sum_i d_i^2,\qquad
 t^2(t-1)\ge\left(\sum_i d_i\right)^2.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The vertices at distances zero, one and two from $v$ are distinct.
There are $1+d_v+\sum_{u\sim v}(d_u-1)=1+\sum_{u\sim v}d_u$ of them.
Summing the inequality $t\ge1+\sum_{u\sim v}d_u$ over $v$ gives the
first assertion; Cauchy's inequality gives the second. Isolated vertices
and disconnected graphs require no separate assumption.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
A simple $5$-regular graph of girth $5$ with exactly fourteen pentagons
through each edge has order at least $48$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix a root $v$. Its radius-two ball has five first neighbours and twenty
leaves in five branches, in addition to $v$. An edge between leaves joins
different branches, and is equivalent to a pentagon through $v$.
Counting those pentagons at the five incident edges gives $5\cdot14/2=35$
leaf edges. The twenty leaves have $20\cdot4=80$ incidences after their
parent edges are removed. Exactly $70$ of these are used by leaf edges,
leaving ten edges from the ball to its complement $Y$.

Put $t=|Y|=n-26$. Regularity gives $2|E(Y)|=5t-10$, so $t\ge2$.
Also $n$, and therefore $t$, is even. The case $t=2$ is excluded by
Lemma~\ref{twenty-eight}. If $n<48$, the remaining possibilities satisfy
$4\le t\le20$. Lemma~\ref{exterior}, applied to the induced graph on $Y$,
gives $t^2(t-1)\ge(5t-10)^2$. This contradicts the identity
\[
 t^2(t-1)-(5t-10)^2
 =(t-4)(t-20)(t-2)-28t+60<0\quad(4\le t\le20).
\]
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Verification and attribution.}
The fixed source is Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of
edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024,
Table~3 on physical page~19. Its row $(5,5,14)$ states lower bound $42$,
with no finite upper bound. We prove the stronger lower bound $48$ and
make no assertion of existence or nonexistence at larger orders.
Cyclotomic irreducibility, field norms, simultaneous diagonalization,
and the Moore count are classical inputs. The author supplies their
combination and the explicit finite certificate here; no priority claim
is made for the order-$28$ obstruction or for the improved bound.
A separate reader checked the already written spectral argument during
development, without supplying a new proof step.

The certificate computes $\Phi_m(4)$ from
$4^m-1=\prod_{d\mid m}\Phi_d(4)$ using exact integer divisions. A separate
polynomial calculation reconstructs each $\Phi_m$ and its real polynomial
$h_m$. It verifies the exact identity
\[4^{\varphi(m)/2}h_m(17/4)=\Phi_m(4).\]
It also checks
all inequalities in Table~\ref{norms}. Literal source graphs of orders
$32$ and $36$ provide $68$ root controls for the ball/exterior identity
and for the disjoint adjacency relation. These controls supplement the
written proof; they are not an enumeration premise. One prior lower-bound
clause is recovered. No new exact-count, whole-original, open-question,
Lean or complexity credit is claimed.
